---
import PhotoPlaceholder from '../PhotoPlaceholder.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section class="band-cream section">
  <SectionHead num="10 / 14 — Context" heading="The shift change." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <figure class="photo-explanatory">
      <PhotoPlaceholder
        description="A line of hospitality workers boarding the bus on Las Vegas Boulevard at 6 a.m. or 2 a.m. The actual face of how the Strip moves — uniforms, lunch bags, name tags."
      />
      <figcaption class="long-caption">
        <strong>The face of how Vegas actually moves.</strong> Tens of thousands of housekeepers, dishwashers,
        line cooks, security officers, valet drivers, dealers, and engineers come off the Strip floor
        at every shift change and head home. Most of them ride the bus. The same is true of every hospital,
        every campus at UNLV and CSN, every downtown office, every active construction site. <em
          >The 14th-busiest bus system in the country is in the Valley because Las&nbsp;Vegas's
          economy literally runs on it.</em
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        The version of this that breaks is when the bus doesn't come. When the casino has to
        schedule shifts around forty-five-minute headways. When the nurse can't take the job because
        there's no late-night route. When the construction company loses crews because the corridor
        they're working never had a bus stop. The cost of bad transit is paid by the businesses that
        depend on workers showing up — and ultimately by the residents who depend on those
        businesses staying open.
      </p>
      <p>
        Transit is not a charity for people without cars. It is the infrastructure that lets a
        workforce of two million people show up to a regional economy. We defend it. Then we build
        the version that doesn't just barely hold the Valley together, but actively grows it — by
        making it easy for the hospital, the campus, and the next downtown employer to expand on the
        assumption that workers can get there.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="vision-tail">
      The Valley's economy already runs on transit. The work is making it run well.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .photo-explanatory {
    margin-block: 3rem;
    margin-inline: 0;
  }
  .long-caption {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--vp-text-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .long-caption strong {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--vp-text);
  }
  .long-caption em {
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
